Task 1: Create and Delete Hard Links
Subtask 1.1: Create a Sample File
Open a terminal and create a working directory:
mkdir link_lab && cd link_lab
Create a sample file with some content:
echo "This is the original file." > original.txt
Verify the file creation:
cat original.txt
Expected Output:
This is the original file.
Subtask 1.2: Create a Hard Link
Use the ln command to create a hard link:
ln original.txt hardlink.txt
Verify the hard link creation:
ls -li
Expected Outcome:
Both original.txt and hardlink.txt will show the same inode number, indicating they point to the same data block.
Subtask 1.3: Test Hard Link Behavior
Modify the content via the hard link:
echo "Appended via hard link." >> hardlink.txt
Check both files:
cat original.txt
cat hardlink.txt
Expected Outcome:
Both files will show the same updated content.
Subtask 1.4: Delete the Hard Link
Remove the hard link:
rm hardlink.txt
Verify the original file still exists:
cat original.txt
Expected Outcome:
The original file remains intact.
Task 2: Create and Delete Symbolic (Soft) Links
Subtask 2.1: Create a Symbolic Link
Create a symbolic link to original.txt:
ln -s original.txt symlink.txt
Verify the symbolic link:
ls -l
Expected Outcome:
symlink.txt will show as a symbolic link pointing to original.txt.
Subtask 2.2: Test Symbolic Link Behavior
Access the file via the symbolic link:

cat symlink.txt
Expected Outcome:
The content of original.txt is displayed.

Delete the original file:

rm original.txt
Attempt to access the symbolic link:

cat symlink.txt
Expected Outcome:
An error (No such file or directory) appears because the symbolic link is now broken.

Subtask 2.3: Recreate and Delete the Symbolic Link
Recreate the original file:
echo "New content." > original.txt
Verify the symbolic link works again:
cat symlink.txt
Remove the symbolic link:
rm symlink.txt
Task 3: Manage Links Across Filesystems
Subtask 3.1: Attempt to Create a Hard Link Across Filesystems
Identify a different filesystem (e.g., /tmp is often a separate mount):
df -h /tmp
Try creating a hard link to a file in another filesystem:
ln original.txt /tmp/hardlink_fail.txt
Expected Outcome:
Error: Invalid cross-device link (hard links cannot span filesystems).
Subtask 3.2: Create a Symbolic Link Across Filesystems
Create a symbolic link to a file in another filesystem:
ln -s $(pwd)/original.txt /tmp/symlink_success.txt
Verify the symbolic link:
cat /tmp/symlink_success.txt
Expected Outcome:
The content of original.txt is displayed.
